= Red Tea (紅茶 / Western "Black Tea")

_Same species (_Camellia sinensis_); the differentiation is by_ full oxidation _of the leaf — the most-oxidized of the four tea categories before microbial post-fermentation. *Crucial cross-cultural naming disambiguation:* the Chinese 紅茶 (hóngchá, "red tea") and the Western "black tea" are the_ same thing — _the Chinese name describes the_ red-amber color of the brewed liquor, _the Western name describes the_ dark color of the dried leaf. _The Chinese 黑茶 (hēichá, "black tea") is_ a different category entirely: _it refers to_ post-fermented dark teas _including pu-erh — discussed in the pu-erh entry. The Western adoption of "black tea" for 紅茶 thus produces a name-collision with the Chinese 黑茶 category; the present entry uses *Chinese 紅茶 / Western black tea* throughout to keep the two distinct. Like oolong, red tea_ postdates the Bencao Gangmu (1578) _as a named category — the canonical first red tea, Lapsang Souchong (正山小種), is a late-Ming Wuyi invention. Ayurveda: not classical (but the modern Indian Assam / Darjeeling industry produces the world's largest volume of red / black tea). Greco-Roman / European: not classical; but red / black tea is the_ pre-eminent Western tea _— the British Empire commodity, the basis of teatime, the standard "tea" in English-speaking culture._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Camellia sinensis_ — see green tea entry; for red tea, both var. _sinensis_ (Fujian and most Chinese red teas) and var. _assamica_ (Yunnan dianhong, Indian Assam, Sri Lankan Ceylon) are used; *var. assamica is the dominant variety for the high-volume Indian / Sri Lankan / African red-tea industry*, while the classical Chinese Wuyi / Qimen red teas use var. _sinensis_.

*English:* *_Black tea_* — the standard Western name; reflects the color of the dried, oxidized leaf, which is dark brown to nearly black. The name has been Western standard since the 17th c.; the conceptually-cleaner "red tea" is also occasionally used (and was used by some 19th-c. British tea-traders who knew the Chinese category name).

*Cross-cultural naming clash:* *_red tea_* in *Western* usage now usually refers to *rooibos* (_Aspalathus linearis_, a South African Fabaceae shrub, not a Camellia at all and *not a true tea*); some Western specialty shops also use "red tea" for Chinese 紅茶 in deliberate parallel to Chinese usage. *_Black tea_* in *Chinese* usage (黑茶 hēichá) refers to *post-fermented dark teas* including pu-erh — discussed separately. The full disambiguation table:

| English | Chinese name | What it is |
|---|---|---|
| Black tea (Western) | 紅茶 (hóngchá) | Fully oxidized Camellia tea — *this entry* |
| Red tea (specialty Western) | 紅茶 (hóngchá) | Same as above, by Chinese name |
| Red tea (popular Western) | — | Rooibos, _Aspalathus linearis_ — *not a Camellia, not in this entry* |
| Dark tea / Hei cha | 黑茶 (hēichá) | Post-fermented Camellia tea (incl. pu-erh) — see _pu-erh-tea.typ_ |

The disambiguation is one of the standard cross-cultural confusion points in the tea / beverage literature.#footnote[Cross-cultural naming clash: Mair & Hoh, _The True History of Tea_ (Thames & Hudson, 2009); standard tea-history literature notes the issue. The rooibos / "red tea" Western re-naming is well documented in modern beverage marketing. _Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "black tea", "red tea", "rooibos").]

*Chinese:* 紅茶 (hóngchá, "red tea") — formal Chinese category name in the six-tea-category system (六大茶類). Specific famous red teas:
- *正山小種 (Zhèngshān Xiǎozhǒng, "Original-Mountain Small-Cultivar")* — anglicized *Lapsang Souchong* (the Min Nan reading). Wuyi mountains, Fujian; the *canonical first red tea*, traditionally pine-smoked over Wuyi pine fires giving a distinctive smoky aroma. The progenitor of all red teas.
- *祁門紅茶 (Qímén Hóngchá)* — anglicized *Keemun*. Anhui province (Qimen county); known for honeyed, orchid, malt notes; the *British "English Breakfast tea" tradition's classical reference*.
- *滇紅 (Diānhóng)* — Yunnan red tea, made from var. _assamica_ large-leaf cultivars; rich, malty, golden-tipped; a more recent (early 20th c.) Chinese red-tea category.
- *金駿眉 (Jīn Jùnméi, "Golden Brave Eyebrows")* — Wuyi mountains, Fujian; a modern (2005) ultra-premium bud-only red tea; market boom in the late 2000s.
- *英德紅茶 (Yīngdé Hóngchá)* — Guangdong; modern industrial red tea.

*Indian, Sri Lankan, African:* the British colonial-era cultivation produced the modern *Assam, Darjeeling, Ceylon (Sri Lanka), Nilgiri (south India), Kenyan, Malawi* black-tea industries; these have their own naming conventions (estate / origin / grade — Orange Pekoe, BOP, etc., the *tea-grade* alphabetic system) that are *not* part of the Chinese tradition.#footnote[Famous Chinese and South Asian red teas: Mair & Hoh; Heiss & Heiss, _The Story of Tea_ (Ten Speed Press, 2007); Hicks, _The Production of Commercial Tea_. The Wuyi pine-smoking tradition is specific to authentic Zhengshan Xiaozhong; modern "Lapsang Souchong" in Western markets often is smoked over different fuels and is a different sub-product.]

*Sanskrit:* No classical entry. The modern Indian _kāli chāy_ (काली चाय, "black tea") is the standard Indian-Hindi reference for red / Western black tea; the chai tradition uses red tea.

== Processing and category definition

Red tea is *fully oxidized* — the cells are bruised / rolled, and the leaf is allowed to oxidize *to completion* (1–3 hours typically, sometimes longer), during which catechins are oxidized to *theaflavins* (TF1–4, the red-orange pigments) and further polymerized to *thearubigins* (the red-brown pigments). The full oxidation is then arrested by *firing* (high-heat drying). The canonical sequence:
1. *Pluck* (採摘) — usually mature whole leaves with bud.
2. *Wither* (萎凋) — air-wither, reducing moisture to ~60%; *no kill-green at this stage*.
3. *Roll / bruise* (揉捻) — ruptures cell walls, mixes polyphenols with polyphenol oxidase.
4. *Full oxidation* (發酵, "fermentation" — a misnomer; this is enzymatic oxidation, not microbial fermentation; the Chinese term is the same as is used for true fermentation, which is a real source of historical confusion) — 1–3 hours under controlled humidity.
5. *Fire / dry* (烘乾) — high-heat drying arrests oxidation and stabilizes the leaf.
6. *Sort and pack.* (For Lapsang Souchong, an *additional pine-fire smoking step* during drying.)

Pharmacologically, full oxidation *almost completely depletes catechins* (especially EGCG drops to a small fraction of the green-tea content) and *generates theaflavins and thearubigins* as the dominant polyphenols.#footnote[Processing technical detail: Hicks, _The Production of Commercial Tea_; the catechin / theaflavin / thearubigin oxidation pathway is foundational tea chemistry. The Chinese 發酵 ("fermentation") naming for enzymatic oxidation is a long-standing terminological problem in Western tea-science translation; technically only the pu-erh / 黑茶 process is true microbial fermentation; modern Chinese tea-science distinguishes 氧化 (oxidation) from 後發酵 (post-fermentation), though the older usage persists.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_Like oolong, red tea postdates the Bencao Gangmu (1578); the modern Chinese-medical placement is from Qing-era and modern tea-dietetics literature, not from the major early Bencao. Lapsang Souchong is a late-Ming Wuyi invention (c. 1600 traditionally; some scholars place it slightly later in the early Qing)._

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* *Warm* (溫) — *the warmest of the four oxidation-defined teas* before pu-erh; the full oxidation has transformed cooling green-tea catechins into warming theaflavins / thearubigins. The thermal classification is the *opposite* of green tea's, on the same plant — a clear demonstration of saṃskāra-creates-substance.

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Sweet (甘), with malty, fruity, honey notes characteristic to the cultivar / region.

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Heart (心); some readings add Lung (肺) and Kidney (腎).

*Actions & indications:*
- 暖胃 / 溫中 — warms the Stomach / warms the middle (the principal warming-classification action; for cold-pattern dyspepsia, cold-related abdominal discomfort).
- 助消化 — aids digestion (especially of fatty / heavy meals; the British / Indian / Chinese practice of red tea after meat-and-fat-heavy meals has cross-cultural confirmation).
- 提神 — refreshes the spirit (the caffeine-and-theaflavin support).
- 利尿 — promotes urination (mild diuretic; like all teas).
- 生熱 — warms / generates internal heat (the modern Chinese functional-food framing of red tea as the "winter" or "cold-constitution" tea).
- 抗氧化 / 養心 — antioxidant / cardiotonic (modern restatement; theaflavin / thearubigin pharmacology).

*Cautions:* contraindicated in heat-pattern conditions, blazing fire, hypertension with internal heat, peptic ulcer with heat; the *warming character means red tea is the wrong tea for inflammatory / heat-pattern conditions, where green tea would be indicated.* Less iron-tannin binding than green tea (the theaflavin / thearubigin polyphenols are *less* iron-binding than catechins — a clinically relevant difference), although still substantial when consumed with meals.#footnote[The warming classification of red tea is the Qing-era and modern Chinese tea-dietetic consensus; the cross-system convergence with the Indian masala chai cultural tradition (warming tea with warming spices) is real. The reduced iron-binding of theaflavins relative to catechins: published nutrition-chemistry literature.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_No classical Sanskrit entry. The reasoned placement parallels the green-tea reasoning but with the_ warming _and_ less drying _profile of full oxidation. Modern Indian dravyaguna treats red / black tea as the typical "chāy" reference._

*Reasoned rasa:* Madhura (मधुर, sweet — moderate, from the malty fruity notes), Tikta (तिक्त, bitter — less than green tea), Kaṣāya (कषाय, astringent — less than green tea, the theaflavin / thearubigin are *less astringent* than catechins).

*Reasoned guṇa:* Laghu (लघु, light), Snigdha (स्निग्ध, slightly unctuous — distinctive shift from green tea's rūkṣa, due to the polymerized theaflavins acquiring slight smooth-mouthfeel), Tīkṣṇa (तीक्ष्ण, sharp — from caffeine).

*Reasoned vīrya:* Uṣṇa (उष्ण, warming) — converges with the Chinese reading. *Red tea is the warming tea.*

*Reasoned vipāka:* Madhura (मधुर, sweet) — with the malty caramel notes.

*Reasoned doṣa effect:* Pacifies Vāta (the snigdha-uṣṇa-madhura profile is *better* for Vāta than green tea's laghu-rūkṣa-śīta); pacifies Kapha in moderate doses (the warming character clears Kapha damp); aggravates Pitta (the warming and the caffeine). The *Vāta-friendly* character is the key dravyaguna distinction from green tea — and is why the *Indian masala chai* (warm milk + red tea + warming spices) is the canonical Vāta-pacifying tea preparation.

*Reasoned karma:* _Dīpana, pācana_ (notably the after-meal digestive use is well-supported across traditions), _medhya_, _kaphahara_ (warming Kapha-clearing), _vāta-anulomana_ (the warm-spiced masala chai preparation is particularly Vāta-anulomana), _ucchvāsa-śuddhi_, _hṛd-rakṣaka_ (modern). In excess, _pitta-prakopa_, _amlapitta_ (heartburn), _nidrā-nāśana_.#footnote[Reasoned dravyaguna; the masala chai cultural-medical preparation is the right register. P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II, treats kāphī (coffee) in a similar warming-stimulant register; red tea sits adjacent but with the snigdha modifier from the oxidized polyphenols.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional, M = modern/pharmacological)_

- *(F) Folk:* same gut-rules as green tea — don't drink on empty stomach (less risk than green tea, but real); the Indian masala chai tradition's milk-and-spice accompaniment moderates many of these concerns.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Caffeine load:* ~40–60 mg per cup; somewhat higher than green tea (cup-for-cup) because of greater leaf-mass extraction in typical Western brewing; less than coffee.
  - *Iron absorption:* still reduces non-heme iron absorption, but less so than green tea (theaflavins / thearubigins are less iron-binding than catechins); important in iron-deficiency anaemia. *Milk addition to tea (the British / Indian practice) partially binds the tannins and further reduces iron interference — the reverse-engineering rationale for milk-tea*.
  - *Drug interactions:* less concentrated EGCG (most has been oxidized); fewer pharmacological interactions than green tea.
  - *Heated milk + tannin in heavy-cream UK style:* some authors raise concerns about milk-protein binding of polyphenols and reducing the cardiovascular benefit; clinical evidence on this is *modest and inconsistent*.
  - *Acidic taste / GERD:* heavily brewed red tea is slightly acidic and astringent; can worsen reflux in heat-pattern reflux.
  - *Pregnancy:* limit caffeine.
  - *Fluoride:* tea-leaf fluoride accumulation similar to green tea.#footnote[Tannin-iron binding for theaflavins vs. catechins: published nutrition-chemistry literature; theaflavins less binding than EGCG. Milk-tannin binding: mixed clinical evidence; the British milk-in-tea tradition has been studied as both reducing and not reducing cardiovascular benefit, with mixed results.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

Red tea (Chinese 紅茶 / Western "black tea") is *not classical in medieval Tibb* but is the dominant tea form in the modern Arabic, Iranian, Maghrebi, Turkish, and South-Asian Muslim cultural worlds. See _green-tea.typ_ for the broader tea-and-Tibb framework. The modern *shāy aḥmar* / *chā'y siyāh* ("red / black tea") is the canonical tea of Egyptian, Levantine, Turkish, Iranian, Afghan, and Pakistani tea-culture; the Maghrebi *Tuareg "Sahara tea"* (gunpowder green-tea-based) is a regional exception. The Iranian samovar / *istikān* (small glass cup) tradition uses red tea. Modern Persian Tibb-e Sonnati and South-Asian Unani treat red tea as *Hot-Neutral and Dry* in the 1st degree (the warming character of full oxidation reads as Hot per the Chinese 温 classification convergence); *muqawwī al-mi'da*, *muḥsin al-hawḍm* (after-meal digestive), *muḥarrik al-akhlāṭ* in excess. Caution in *amrāḍ ṣafrāwiyya* (hot-pattern conditions) at heavy consumption; the iron-binding caveat applies. The *Iranian-style red tea with rock sugar (nabāt) and saffron / cardamom*, the *Egyptian / Levantine shāy bi-l-naʿnaʿ* (mint-tea) when made with red base, and the *South-Asian masala chai* (red tea + milk + warming spices, treated in Indian Unani as Vāta-pacifying) are the major cultural-medical preparations. The modern register is rich but the medieval pharmacology is post-classical.

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *單樅 / 工夫 (gōngfu)*-style red-tea preparation — small-cup, short-steep gong-fu cha is also applied to good Chinese red teas (Qimen, Lapsang Souchong, Jin Junmei), although Western large-pot brewing dominates.
- *Cold-process / iced red tea* — modern Chinese functional-food preparation.
- *Red tea + 桂圓 (longan)* — traditional Chinese warming combination for cold-blood / debility states.

=== Ayurveda

- *Masala chai (मसाला चाय)* — *the canonical modern Indian preparation*: red tea (typically Assam or strong CTC tea) boiled in milk and water with crushed ginger, cardamom, cinnamon stick, clove, black pepper, sometimes fennel and bay leaf; sweetened with sugar or jaggery. Not a classical Ayurvedic formula but a reasoned and consistently Vāta-pacifying preparation that *converts a Vāta-aggravating drug (caffeine-tannin tea) into a Vāta-friendly nourishing drink* by spice and milk accompaniment. The traditional preparation is universal across India and is one of the *cleanest cross-system convergences*: the *empirical-folk Indian moderation of the imported tea* parallels the *Ayurvedic theoretical doctrine of saṃskāra-corrective preparation*.
- *Kāli chai* — the plain unsweetened red tea (less common in Indian practice).
- *Adrak chai (अदरक चाय)* — ginger-emphasized chai; Vāta-pacifying.

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

This is red tea's *substantive cultural tradition* (though not a classical-pharmacological one).

- *17th-c. Dutch and British introduction* (see green tea entry for the general European tea introduction); within decades, *red tea displaced green as the dominant Western tea* — likely because the longer storage life of fully-oxidized tea was suited to the long sea voyages from China to Europe.
- *British East India Company (17th–19th c.)* — red tea trade dominates the British Empire's commercial relationship with China.
- *18th-c. British "tea table"* — the social institution; tea + milk + sugar + bread becomes the British working- and middle-class breakfast staple.
- *Catherine of Braganza* (Portuguese-born wife of Charles II of England) popularized tea-drinking at the British court (1660s).
- *Anna, 7th Duchess of Bedford* (1840s) is credited with founding the "afternoon tea" tradition.
- *19th-c. Assam discovery* — Robert Bruce, an East India Company officer, documented wild _C. sinensis_ var. _assamica_ in Assam (1820s); industrial plantation cultivation followed; Assam became (and remains) one of the world's largest tea-producing regions.
- *19th-c. Darjeeling cultivation* — Chinese var. _sinensis_ cultivation in the Himalayan foothills; produces the distinctively muscat-grape-flavored Darjeeling.
- *Sri Lanka (Ceylon)* — coffee blight (1860s) prompted the switch to tea; Ceylon becomes a major black-tea producer.
- *British Pharmacopoeia and medical use:* tea entered European pharmacy in the 18th c. as a mild stimulant and diuretic; never a major drug.
- *Opium Wars (1839–1842, 1856–1860):* British silver outflow to China to pay for tea was the proximate economic cause of the British East India Company's opium-cultivation policy in India and the subsequent Opium Wars.
- *Modern Western:* red / black tea is the canonical Western tea; the breakfast tea, the "tea bag" tea, the standard reference.
- *British "builder's tea"* (strong, hot, with milk and sugar) is the iconic British register.#footnote[Tea and the British Empire: standard imperial history (Pettigrew, _A Social History of Tea_; Macfarlane & Macfarlane, _The Empire of Tea_); Mintz, _Sweetness and Power_ on sugar / tea / slavery; the Opium Wars: standard imperial history.]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Key constituents:*
- *Caffeine:* ~2.5–4% of dry leaf; cup-for-cup somewhat higher caffeine extraction than green tea due to brewing practice.
- *Theaflavins (TF1, TF2A, TF2B, TF3):* ~1–4% of dry leaf; the red-orange pigment / antioxidant fraction; *characteristic of red tea*.
- *Thearubigins:* ~10–20% of dry leaf; the red-brown high-molecular-weight polymer fraction; the dominant polyphenol class in red tea.
- *Residual catechins:* small fraction.
- *L-theanine:* present, similar to green tea.
- *Volatile aromatics:* malty, fruity, honeyed compounds — methional, phenylacetaldehyde, geraniol, linalool, β-damascenone (the rose-honey note in Keemun), and many others.
- *Maillard products* from the firing step.
- *Fluoride:* similar to green tea.#footnote[Red-tea chemistry: standard pharmacognosy (Evans); reviews in _Journal of Agricultural and Food Chemistry_. The theaflavin / thearubigin biochemistry: foundational tea-chemistry literature (Robertson, _Tea: Cultivation to Consumption_).]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Caffeine:* applies fully; standard pharmacology.
- *Theaflavins:* have their own antioxidant and lipid-lowering pharmacology, distinct from EGCG. Modest cardiovascular evidence.
- *Theaflavin-3,3'-digallate (TF3):* preclinical evidence for various activities; clinical translation preliminary.
- *Cardiovascular:* observational data and small RCTs suggest modest improvements in cholesterol, blood-pressure, and endothelial function with regular red-tea consumption; effect sizes modest.
- *Glycemic:* mild improvements in glycemic control in regular consumers.
- *Cognitive:* caffeine + L-theanine; alertness, attention.
- *Antibacterial:* in vitro broad-spectrum; modest clinical relevance.
- *Iron-binding:* less than green tea but still meaningful.
- *Cross-system convergence:* the warming / digestive / cardiovascular axis of the classical Chinese / modern Indian / British dietetic tradition aligns with the modern caffeine-+-theaflavin pharmacology. The popular health-claims for red tea are *more modest than for green tea's EGCG* (because the Western marketing focus has been on green tea), and as a consequence the red-tea evidence base is *less inflated*.#footnote[Clinical evidence for red tea: cardiovascular meta-analyses (modest effects); glycemic studies (modest); cognitive studies (consistent with caffeine + theanine); verify against current systematic reviews. The less-inflated marketing claim is a real observation.]

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Brewed red tea:* 3–5 cups/day (~600–1000 mL) is the typical moderate range; well-tolerated.
- *Masala chai:* the milk-spice preparation moderates the caffeine and tannin effects; the standard 2–4 cups/day Indian register.
- *British "builder's tea":* the strong brewed tea + milk + sugar — caloric and tannic; same caffeine guidance applies.
- *Risk groups:* same as green tea — iron-deficiency anaemia (less of an issue, but real), pregnancy (limit caffeine), arrhythmia, insomnia, anxiety, peptic ulcer, severe acid reflux.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*

